        <p>Read the last column top to bottom, because the pattern is the point of this section: <strong>the fewer hours you work, the more each hour keeps</strong>. That’s $43.88 at 20 hours a week against $40.83 at 40, a $3.05-an-hour gap on the same $52.00 rate. The reason is the bracket. None of a 20-hour year’s $37,980.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket, while a 25-hour year’s $51,500.00 of taxable income runs $1,100.00 into it, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up at all. Three things in that table matter more than the headline. First, the 37.5-hour paid week, common in offices that pay for a 7.5-hour day, is $101,400.00 gross, which still beats a $95,000 salary by $6,400.00 gross and $4,502.40 of take-home. A 2.5-hour shorter week costs $6,760.00 gross and $4,755.66 of take-home. Second, the step from 30 to 40 hours is worth $27,040.00 gross and $19,022.64 of take-home ($1,585.22 a month). That is the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Third, part-time keeps a bigger share: at 20 hours you keep 84.4% of gross, against 78.5% full-time. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
