        <p>The premium is the part people overcount. Of the $20,280.00, $13,520.00 is straight-time pay those hours would have earned anyway (5 × 52 × $52.00), and only $6,760.00 is the extra half-rate (5 × 52 × $26.00). The take-home tells the same story honestly: $99,054.74 is $14,134.18 more than the no-overtime $84,920.56, because the overtime year pushes $55,300.00 of taxable income into the 22% bracket and another $6,640.00 into the 24% bracket, costing $1,593.60. One extra overtime hour a week, every week, is the smaller version of the same deal: $4,056.00 gross, $112,216.00 for the year, and $87,773.96 of take-home, $2,853.40 more kept. Ten overtime hours a week doubles the pattern, $148,720.00 gross and $112,916.12 take-home, and it quietly crosses a second line: $26,920.00 of that year’s taxable income is taxed at 24%, costing $6,460.80. At $51 an hour it took five overtime hours a week to reach the 24% bracket at all, with $4,170.00 of income costing $1,000.80. At $52 the same five hours push $6,640.00 into 24%, costing $1,593.60. Two warnings before you count on it. Overtime is per workweek, never per pay period: a 45-hour week next to a 35-hour week still pays 5 overtime hours, as our <a href="/articles/how-to-fill-out-a-timesheet/">timesheet guide</a> prices in dollars. And in five states daily overtime adds a second threshold on top of the weekly one, which our <a href="/articles/which-states-have-daily-overtime/">daily-overtime guide</a> maps state by state. Our <a href="/articles/what-is-double-time-pay/">double-time guide</a> shows what happens when the multiplier is 2× instead of 1.5×, and the <a href="/overtime-calculator/">overtime calculator</a> prices any hours pattern you enter.</p>
